    category: "Frontend Supply Chain Security",
    expectedAnswer: "**npm security** is a frontend supply-chain security concern. The goal is to reduce the chance that an untrusted or compromised dependency, script, package, or build artifact becomes executable code in the application.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the concept.\n\n**npm security** is a frontend supply-chain security concern. The goal is to reduce the chance that an untrusted or compromised dependency, script, package, or build artifact becomes executable code in the application.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nDefine the protected asset, attacker capability, trust boundary, secure default, enforcement point, and recovery path before writing code.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual example exposes the core policy or state check so you can explain the security reasoning line by line during the interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface Dependency {\n  name: string;\n  version: string;\n  approved: boolean;\n}\n\nfunction approvedDependencies(\n  dependencies: readonly Dependency[],\n): Dependency[] {\n  const result: Dependency[] = [];\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < dependencies.length; i += 1) {\n    if (dependencies[i].approved) {\n      result.push(dependencies[i]);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n\nconst approved = approvedDependencies([\n  { name: \"react\", version: \"19.0.0\", approved: true },\n  { name: \"demo-package\", version: \"1.0.0\", approved: false },\n]);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collections or browser APIs when they make the same control clearer without hiding the security boundary.\n\n```ts\nconst dependencies = [\n  { name: \"react\", version: \"19.0.0\", approved: true },\n  { name: \"demo-package\", version: \"1.0.0\", approved: false },\n];\n\nconst approved = dependencies.filter(\n  (dependency) => dependency.approved,\n);\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse npm security in the dependency/build pipeline. Approve packages explicitly, preserve lockfile/provenance evidence, and block known high-risk artifacts before release.\n\nStep 6 — Validation:\nTest the intended path, denied path, malformed input, missing/expired state, misconfiguration, and recovery/rollback behavior where applicable.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain what is trusted, what is untrusted, where final enforcement occurs, why the default is safe, and how monitoring or testing prevents recurrence.",
    productionExample: "Use npm security in the dependency/build pipeline. Approve packages explicitly, preserve lockfile/provenance evidence, and block known high-risk artifacts before release.",
    bestPractices: ["Define the asset, attacker capability, and trust boundary first.", "Enforce security-sensitive decisions at a trusted boundary.", "Prefer least privilege, explicit allow-lists, and secure defaults.", "Do not log passwords, tokens, secrets, or unnecessary personal data.", "Test both successful and denied/invalid paths."],
    tradeOffs: "The manual/core version is intentionally explicit for interview explanation. The built-in/browser version is shorter for production. Prefer the approach that preserves a clear security boundary, least privilege, and maintainability.",
